00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.550
comes out of it.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.490
So let's get going.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.090
I have to stay on this
side, I think I do.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.870
Oh, there we go.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:57.730
Excellent.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:03.280
So the behavior of animals
in the natural world, where

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.460
they're free to do what
they want, when they want,

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.961
is obviously remarkably complex.

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:10.419
And it's because
of this complexity

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.030
that to us, as humans, it often
appears that their behavior is

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:14.770
fundamentally inscrutable.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:18.310
But of course, the ethologists
working 50 to 60 years ago

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:22.780
hypothesize that spontaneous
naturalistic behavior like this

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.630
actually has a ton of
underlying structure.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:28.270
They believed that spontaneous
behavior is actually

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.660
composed out of a series
of behavioral modules,

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:36.280
brief three-dimensional,
stereotyped, repeated units

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.910
or motifs of action or movement
that are concatenated one

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:44.080
after the other in time to build
complex behavioral sequences

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.990
and which are organized
hierarchically in time, thereby

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.950
affording behavior a kind of
spatiotemporal organization

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.800
that's selected for by
evolution and allows animals

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.630
both to kind of deal
with the challenges

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:57.400
of their particular
ecological niche,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.380
but also enables them
just enough flexibility

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.310
to adapt to changes in
context and contingency.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.530
They also made an argument
that I think all of us

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.070
will find very intuitive,
which is that, if indeed there

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.440
is a kind of underlying
structure to behavior,

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.200
it must somehow arise
in the nervous system.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.630
But despite the fact that
this sentence was written now

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:20.200
nearly 60 years ago, we
still, I would argue,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.660
have only the most
superficial understanding

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.787
of how the brain composes
spontaneous behavior.

00:03:25.787 --> 00:03:28.120
And by spontaneous behavior,
I mean the kind of behavior

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.800
that you and I and
almost every creature

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.560
spends most of our day doing,
the kind of behavior that

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.660
happens in the
absence of a task,

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:36.790
without obvious and
explicit reward,

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.280
sometimes without obvious
goals, the kind of behavior

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.680
that happens when
you're not confined

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.100
behaviourally or
psychologically or physically.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.490
This question, how the brain
composes naturalistic behavior,

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:51.080
has really haunted my
lab for the last decade.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.060
And so to begin to
address this question,

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.290
we've been building tools
that allow us to characterize

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.420
the underlying modular
basis for mouse

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.430
behavior in the laboratory.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.470
And the idea is that if
we can begin to do that,

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:05.620
we can begin to test this
kind of ethological hypothesis

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.470
that behavior is
built out of parts.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:10.450
The tool we've built and
we've been working on

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.390
is called motion sequencing,
or MoSeq for short.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.170
We've published a bunch
on this technique,

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:16.628
and so I'm not
going to describe it

00:04:16.628 --> 00:04:18.190
to you in any
great detail at all

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.570
because I want to show
you just the data.

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:24.550
But at a very high level, MoSeq
is an unsupervised machine

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:27.610
learning algorithm that,
based on the spatial temporal

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.100
structure of
behavioral data alone,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.530
without any human
supervision or intervention,

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:36.010
is capable of discovering
an optimal set

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.460
of behavioral modules, or what
we call behavioral syllables,

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.830
out of which behavior
is composed in any given

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:42.910
experiment.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.280
The behavioral data we tend
to feed motion sequencing

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.980
is 3D imaging of mice as
they explore simple arenas,

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.900
like this circular
open field over here.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.160
And we obtain these 3D images
by placing depth cameras, which

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.160
we buy from Best Buy for
$100, over buckets that we

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.290
get for $5 from Home Depot.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:02.810
And you can see the output
of one of these depth cameras

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:03.310
here.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.185
Here heat mapped over
each pixel of the mouse

00:05:05.185 --> 00:05:07.300
is its height at
any given position,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:08.845
and you can very
much get the sense

00:05:08.845 --> 00:05:11.470
that these cameras, even though
they're imperfect in many ways,

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.590
give you a rich representation
of the animals ongoing three

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.030
dimensional pose dynamics.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:20.200
And again, we feed these 3D data
to the MoSeq algorithm, which

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:25.090
is a generative time series
model for behavior that

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:28.030
instantiates this ethological
hypothesis that behavior is

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:31.570
composed out of modules that
tumble, one after the other,

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:32.950
somewhat predictably in time.

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:35.890
And by fitting this model
through unsupervised

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:39.310
techniques, MoSeq discovers
that this kind of parts

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:40.420
list for behavior.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.460
In a typical open
field experiment,

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.530
it discovers maybe 40 or 50
of these behavioral syllables,

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.250
which correspond to human
identifiable behaviors, things

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:51.620
like pauses, dives, and runs.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.580
The average duration of each
one of these little behavioral

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.190
syllables is 300,
400, 500 milliseconds,

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:02.950
and so MoSeq teaches us about a
kind of sub-second architecture

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:05.140
that seems to be characteristic
of mouse behavior.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.360
And we can synthesize
for any given experiment

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.580
the entire output of
motion sequencing into one

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.080
of these behavioral state maps.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:13.488
Here, each node is a
behavioral syllable.

00:06:13.488 --> 00:06:15.280
The diameter of each
one of these syllables

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.500
represents how often
within an experiment

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:19.390
that particular
syllable is used.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.180
And the thickness of these
directional yellow arrows

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.340
indicates the
observed likelihood

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:27.820
of transition over time from one
behavioral syllable to the next

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.210
during the experiment.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:31.120
And so I hope you can
tell just by inspection

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.580
that the behavior of
mice in the laboratory

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:37.030
is captured by MoSeq, just
like the behavior of mice out

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:39.670
in the real world
is characterized

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.333
by having an enormous
amount of structure.

00:06:42.333 --> 00:06:43.750
And the question
we want to answer

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:46.430
is, where does the
structure come from?

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:48.520
How does the brain build this?

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.310
And that has got us thinking
about parts of the brain that

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:52.710
are involved in
building behavior

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.560
and specific corticobasal
ganglia loops,

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:58.442
and in particular the input
nucleus to the basal ganglia.

00:06:58.442 --> 00:07:00.150
That's the part of
the brain here in blue

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:03.300
called the striatum because
of its well known roles

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.940
both in action selection
and in motor control, which,

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:07.680
of course, are going
to be functions

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.800
that are critical to composing
spontaneous naturalistic

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:12.030
behavior.

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:14.280
We've focused on a specific
subregion of the striatum,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.718
a part of the striatum called
the dorsolateral striatum.

00:07:16.718 --> 00:07:18.010
That's for a couple of reasons.

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:20.790
First, Dorsolateral
Striatum, or DLS,

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:24.180
gets inputs from
proprioceptive motor cortices,

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.190
suggesting the dorsolateral
striatum has access

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:29.190
to the kind of information you'd
want if you wanted to organize

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.520
your 3D post dynamics, but
also because work by others,

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.410
in particular Kent
Berridge, has suggested

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.590
that during simple spontaneous
behaviors, like grooming,

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.140
the dorsolateral striatum
might play an important role

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.430
kind of in organizing
those behaviors.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.490
And so for all those
reasons, we wanted

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:47.280
to test the hypothesis
that maybe the DLS contains

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.280
within it neural correlates
for behavioral syllables,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.640
behavioral grammar, the
sequence in which syllables

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.440
are expressed, or both.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:56.940
So to address this
question, we have

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.070
rendered, together
with our collaborators,

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.680
motion sequencing
compatible with all forms

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:02.730
of neural recording.

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.880
And so now we can use MoSeq
as a general purpose platform

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:08.070
for querying brain
behavior relationships

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.570
during spontaneous behaviors.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:12.150
So let me show you an
example of us doing this.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:13.600
That's shown here.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.710
So here, on the
upper left, is going

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:19.540
to be a mouse running around
in one of these buckets.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:20.790
This is obviously 3D image.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:22.908
Here we're going to
extract the movie, the 3D

00:08:22.908 --> 00:08:23.700
movie of the mouse.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.890
We're going to align it so
its nose is to the right

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:28.650
and its tail is to the left,
and we're going to center

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.190
it in this image.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.059
You can tell that this
mouse is wearing a hat out

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:34.710
of which is coming
a cable, and that's

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.210
because we've been
planted in miniscope

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:37.585
within its
dorsolateral striatum.

00:08:37.585 --> 00:08:39.750
And you can see what the
shape is visualizing here.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:41.520
Here you're looking
at calcium transients

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.530
and individual spiny
projection neurons

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:44.880
in dorsolateral striatum.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.585
And here in the center, we can
line these two data streams up.

00:08:47.585 --> 00:08:48.960
Here on the bottom,
we're looking

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.270
at calcium transients and
individual spiny projection

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:52.350
neurons in DLS.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:55.110
And up above is our
post hoc identification

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:58.990
of each behavioral
syllable as is expressed.

00:08:58.990 --> 00:08:59.938
Sorry.

00:08:59.938 --> 00:09:02.230
I'm a little bit under the
weather, so I just want to--

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:02.730
perfect.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.847
And so what you can see,
even just by inspection,

00:09:07.847 --> 00:09:10.180
is that there seems to be
some interesting relationships

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:13.600
between population level
activity in these neurons

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.720
and the behavior of the animals
express as they freely behave.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.850
It's as if, as animals
switch from one

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.575
behavioral syllable
to the next, there's

00:09:20.575 --> 00:09:23.200
a switching in the ensembles of
neurons that are activated such

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:24.760
that a new ensemble
of neurons is

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.782
kind of dialed in as
the animal switches

00:09:26.782 --> 00:09:28.490
from one behavioral
syllable to the next.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:30.128
And if we formally
analyze the data,

00:09:30.128 --> 00:09:31.670
that's exactly what
seems to be true.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:34.390
And indeed, if we look at
the ensembles themselves,

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.610
it turns out we can
use the identity

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.130
of each syllable-specific
neural ensemble

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:42.220
to make accurate predictions
about the specific 3D

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:45.200
behavior the animal's expressing
it at any given moment in time.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:46.450
In other words, we
can kind of look down

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:48.158
at the animal's brain,
and I can tell you

00:09:48.158 --> 00:09:50.930
which syllable it's expressing
on a moment by moment basis.

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:53.650
In addition, this neural data
contains within an information

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:56.830
about the specific
sequence context in which

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:58.370
each syllable is expressed.

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.270
And if we lesion the
dorsolateral striatum,

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:04.030
animals continue to
appropriately express

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.710
behavioral syllables from
a kinematic perspective.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:08.980
But their ordering
is totally incorrect,

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:10.660
preventing them
from generating kind

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.950
of adaptive
sensory-driven behaviors.

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:15.610
And so what this tells
us is that, indeed,

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:17.350
the dorsolateral
striatum seems like it's

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:19.600
an important place
for organizing

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.280
spontaneous
behavioral sequences,

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:24.027
but it fails to tell us
some of the answers to some

00:10:24.027 --> 00:10:26.110
of the key kind of remaining
questions about where

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:27.490
this structure comes from.

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:29.710
For example, why are
some behavioral syllables

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:31.522
used more than others?

00:10:31.522 --> 00:10:33.730
Why do some behavioral
syllables, like this one, tend

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:37.060
to participate in highly
deterministic, very predictable

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.910
behavioral sequences, going
from here to here to here,

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:41.748
whereas other
behavioral syllables

00:10:41.748 --> 00:10:44.290
tend to be connected to a bunch
of other behavioral syllables

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.180
more probabilistically
or randomly,

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:49.150
seemingly participating
in behavioral sequences

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.450
kind of more willy-nilly?

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:52.530
Where does all that
structure come from?

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:53.947
And so thinking
about that problem

00:10:53.947 --> 00:10:57.852
got us thinking about the major
neuromodulator that influences

00:10:57.852 --> 00:10:59.810
the striatum, and that
is, of course, dopamine,

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.350
which is well known to
influence both population level

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:06.490
activity in medium spiny
neurons in the striatum

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:08.980
and to be capable of inducing
plasticity in those very

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:12.670
same neurons, which is why
dopamine has been implicated

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:17.740
in a large number of different
contexts as a teaching signal.

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:20.170
The dopamine that talks
to dorsolateral striatum

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.650
largely arises from neurons
that live in the midbrain,

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:26.960
in substantia nigra pars
compacta, as shown here.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.670
And there's reasonable reason
to believe that dopamine

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:32.590
might play an important
role in organizing

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:34.340
spontaneous behavior.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.930
And we know that from
thinking just about what

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.680
the behaviors that are
changed in patients

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:40.000
with Parkinson's disease.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.630
Those patients have difficulties
in initiating new movements,

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:45.910
in invigorating their
movements, and in appropriately

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:47.625
sequencing their movements.

00:11:47.625 --> 00:11:49.000
That being said,
we actually know

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:50.583
very little about
the role of dopamine

00:11:50.583 --> 00:11:53.210
during spontaneous
behavior in animal models.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:55.180
There's basically
two lines of thought

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:57.650
about what dopamine might
be doing in animal models.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:00.180
The first comes from work of
folks like Rui Costa and Dan

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:03.550
Dombeck, who have observed that,
as animals are stopped and then

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:05.050
generate a new
movement, there tends

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:06.640
to be a little
flicker in dopamine,

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:08.830
suggesting that maybe these
dopamine flickers that

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.260
are associated with movement
initiation are causal.

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.810
And indeed, if you
kind of optogenetically

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:15.670
induce a flood of
dopamine in the striatum,

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:17.650
animals will begin
a new movement.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:19.750
There's another kind of
non-mutually exclusive set

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:21.430
of ideas coming
from Henry In's lab

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:24.010
that maybe dopamine dynamics
are actually quite continuous,

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:26.530
and the continuous
variation in dopamine

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:29.938
actually specifies the animal's
ongoing movement kinematics.

00:12:29.938 --> 00:12:31.480
But again, as I've
mentioned, there's

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:34.810
actually hasn't been that many
careful kind of recordings

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.060
of dopamine as animals are
just spontaneously behaving

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:38.180
in any context.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.380
So the degree to which either
of these is a general rule

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:43.150
remains a little
bit of a mystery.

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:46.222
What is true and what we know a
lot about from 40 years of work

00:12:46.222 --> 00:12:47.930
is something that I've
already mentioned,

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:50.860
which is that dopamine plays
this key role as a teaching

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:55.030
signal and is capable of shaping
animals' future behaviors

00:12:55.030 --> 00:13:00.430
in order to assist animals
in maximizing their ability

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:01.990
to obtain rewards.

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:04.960
But it's hard to think about
the kind of role dopamine

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:07.600
might play as a teaching
signal, helping animals

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:09.813
who obtain reward, in a
context like the open field

00:13:09.813 --> 00:13:11.980
that I showed you, where
an animal is running around

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:17.290
in the dark, with no task, no
goals, and certainly no reward.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:18.832
And so most of our
attention has been

00:13:18.832 --> 00:13:20.748
focused on trying to
understand whether or not

00:13:20.748 --> 00:13:22.300
dopamine during
spontaneous behavior

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:24.950
relates to initiation
or kinematics.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:26.575
So we began thinking
about this, and we

00:13:26.575 --> 00:13:28.783
decided to do a very simple
experiment, which is just

00:13:28.783 --> 00:13:30.770
to repeat the experiment
I showed you before,

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:34.122
but now to record dopamine
transients specifically

00:13:34.122 --> 00:13:35.830
in dorsolateral
striatum, through the use

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.870
of the dopamine reporter dLight.

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:40.000
And so here, we're
doing fiber photometry.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.542
You can see the mouse running
around in the bucket over here.

00:13:42.542 --> 00:13:45.730
And here you're looking
at dopamine transients,

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:48.830
as reported by dLight,
locked to syllables,

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:51.340
which are being
identified post hoc.

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:54.220
And what you can see
just by inspection

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:56.680
is that there are far too
many dopamine transients

00:13:56.680 --> 00:14:00.190
to be explained by
movement initiation alone.

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:03.010
In addition, if we do a
ton of math, which we did,

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:06.010
we have a very difficult
time identifying

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:08.140
any kinematic
parameters that seem

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.750
to be encoded by these
dopamine fluctuations,

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:12.110
with one exception.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:14.240
And that exception is velocity.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:17.350
So what's interesting is that if
we bend the dopamine transients

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:19.030
at a relatively
short time scales,

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:21.130
at timescales of
5 to 10 seconds,

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:23.440
we actually see a
negative correlation

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:25.420
between the amplitude
or number of dopamine

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:27.763
transients the animal's
experiencing and its speed.

00:14:27.763 --> 00:14:29.680
In other words, if there's
a burst of dopamine

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:31.840
during spontaneous
behavior, animals actually

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:33.103
tend to slow down.

00:14:33.103 --> 00:14:35.020
And that kind of weird
counterintuitive result

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:37.030
is something that had been
observed by others in the field

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:37.530
before.

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.155
But what's interesting
is that if we've

00:14:39.155 --> 00:14:41.260
been the data over longer
and longer time scales,

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:43.840
this relationship
actually seems to invert.

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:46.060
And now we have the kind
of comfortable positive

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:48.580
correlation between dopamine
and movement that all of us

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:51.610
were taught kind of in our
neuroscience classes, where

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:54.280
dopamine is thought to kind of
broadly invigorate or enable

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:58.760
in some way the animal's
ability to generate movement.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:14:59.260
OK.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:01.600
What about movement
initiation per se?

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.150
So this is what we see if we
look at movement initiations.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:05.260
The x-axis here is time.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:08.470
Time 0 is the moment at
which the animal generates

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:10.450
a new movement
after being still,

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:12.400
and indeed we can see
a nice little dopamine

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:15.700
flicker, as has been observed
in the past, associated

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:18.100
with the initiation of
a brand new movement.

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:20.110
But the amplitude
of this flicker

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:22.690
is much smaller
than the amplitudes

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:26.200
we observe if we
actually tee off

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:28.820
this data on all
behavioral transitions.

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:30.940
So here, time 0 is
actually the transition

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:34.000
from any syllable into
any other syllable,

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:35.770
so the animal is
moving here and it's

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:38.050
moving here, just that the
movements are different.

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:41.050
And what you can see are these
nice, large amplitude changes

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:43.210
in dopamine that
systematically reflect

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:45.070
when animals switch
from expressing

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:46.600
one behavioral
syllable to the next.

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:50.590
It's as if the dopamine kind of
exhausts itself as the animal

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:53.080
ends one syllable,
and then there's

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.630
this large upsurge in
dopamine as animals

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:58.953
enter into and begin to
express the very next syllable.

00:15:58.953 --> 00:16:00.370
So this is what
we see on average.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:02.860
We see this large systematic
fluctuation in dopamine

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:04.660
across all syllable transitions.

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:06.280
This isn't actually
what we see if we

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:07.852
look at individual syllables.

00:16:07.852 --> 00:16:09.310
If we look at
individual syllables,

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:12.310
we see a lot of variation
in the dopamine amplitudes

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:15.100
that are, on average, associated
with any given syllable.

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:17.980
So here, we're identifying 40
different behavioral syllables

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:18.790
in this experiment.

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:21.415
And if we just look at this very
top behavioral syllable, which

00:16:21.415 --> 00:16:24.130
turns out to be a rear, when
animals begin to express

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:26.770
this rear, dopamine
kind of quickly ramps up

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:29.860
to this very high amplitude and
then characteristically falls.

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:31.750
In contrast, if you
look at this syllable

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:34.150
down here at the bottom,
this turns out to be a pause.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:36.010
When animals express a
pause, their dopamine

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:36.790
tends to be pretty low.

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:38.748
Then slowly during the
expression of the pause,

00:16:38.748 --> 00:16:39.295
it ramps up.

00:16:39.295 --> 00:16:41.170
And if we look at any
one of these syllables,

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:44.290
we'll see associated with
it a characteristic dopamine

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:45.350
waveform.

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:46.750
Now, this might
give you the idea

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:49.180
that we should be able
to use these waveforms

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:53.230
or their amplitudes to predict
which behavioral syllable

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:55.210
the animal's expressing
at any moment in time,

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:56.860
in much the same
way, as I told you,

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:59.500
that if we look at actual neural
activity and spiny projection

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:01.960
neurons, I can make an accurate
predictions about which 3D

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:03.460
behavior animals are expressing.

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:04.990
So if we try to
play that same trick

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:07.599
with these dopamine
transients, it

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:09.890
turns out we can't
make predictions.

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:12.880
I can't tell you which behavior
the animal's expressing

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:15.375
on a moment-by-moment
basis based

00:17:15.375 --> 00:17:16.750
upon the dopamine
waveform that's

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:18.380
associated with that behavior.

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:19.960
And the reason why
becomes obvious

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:22.660
if we just look at
different instances

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:24.890
of this particular
behavioral syllable.

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:27.430
What we find is that there's
a huge amount of variability

00:17:27.430 --> 00:17:31.210
in terms of the amplitude and
waveform in dopamine associated

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:32.320
with any given behavior.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:34.678
Yes, indeed, on some
instances of this rear,

00:17:34.678 --> 00:17:36.970
you see this kind of high
amplitude dopamine transient.

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:38.470
But on many instances,
you actually

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:40.520
see dopamine going in
the opposite direction.

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:43.990
And that holds true for all
of the behavioral syllables.

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:47.320
And so we can't
make predictions on

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:49.960
an instance-by-instance
basis of which syllable's

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:53.330
being expressed based upon
the dopamine waveform.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:56.110
And this is broadly consistent
with my previous claim

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:58.570
that it does not
appear that dopamine

00:17:58.570 --> 00:18:01.040
is encoding the kinematics
of the animal's movement.

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.700
Here are four separate examples
of the animal generating

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:05.750
the same rear, up behavior.

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:07.348
And yet, in all
four of these cases,

00:18:07.348 --> 00:18:08.890
there's a different
dopamine waveform

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:10.810
that's associated with it.

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:14.270
So these dopamine waveforms
can't be encoding kinematics.

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:16.390
So now we're in this
kind of weird situation.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:19.690
It's clear there's a
strong temporal correlation

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:22.720
between dopamine
kind of flickering

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.120
as the animals switch from
one syllable to the next

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:27.455
during spontaneous
behavior, but it's unclear

00:18:27.455 --> 00:18:29.080
what these flickers
mean to the animal.

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:30.663
They certainly don't
encode kinematics

00:18:30.663 --> 00:18:34.340
and they don't encode the
ongoing behavioral syllable.

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:37.420
So in order to kind of
clarify our own thinking,

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:39.830
we did a simple
calibration experiment.

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:42.700
So all we did was we
took some chocolate chips

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:44.410
and we just threw
them into the bucket.

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:46.570
And then we measured
dopamine transients

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:50.260
as animals consumed these
delicious and surprising and

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:51.460
rewarding treats.

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:54.040
And what we observed is that
the dopamine fluctuations that

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:57.910
were elicited when
the animals consumed

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:00.670
these surprising rewards, which
clearly in a different context

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:02.680
were capable of shaping
the animal's behavior,

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:05.680
were of roughly the same
amplitude as the fluctuations

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:08.890
that we were observing
in dorsolateral striatum

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:10.780
during spontaneous behavior.

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:14.290
And that suggested to us a
really surprising possibility.

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:15.910
Maybe these dopamine
fluctuations

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:18.490
aren't actually controlling
the animal's movements

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:19.600
in the moment.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:21.040
Maybe they're
instead controlling

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:23.770
the animal's movements
in the future

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:26.590
by reinforcing in some way
the syllables with which they

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:29.570
are associated on any
given syllable instance.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:32.230
If that is true, you would
make a very simple and basic

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:35.800
prediction, which is that those
syllables that are associated

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:38.680
with the most dopamine on
average during the experiment

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:40.150
are precisely those
syllables that

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:43.240
should be used the most on
average during the experiment.

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:45.940
And in fact, as you can see
here on the left, that's true.

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:47.800
The x-axis here is
dopamine, the y-axis

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:49.420
here is how often
the syllable's used.

00:19:49.420 --> 00:19:51.878
Each dot here is a
syllable-mouse pair,

00:19:51.878 --> 00:19:53.920
and indeed we see this
nice positive correlation,

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:57.040
revealing that those syllables
that are used the most

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:59.290
are those that on average
are associated with the most

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:00.010
dopamine.

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:02.380
And what's super interesting
is that this relationship

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:04.390
between dopamine
and syllable usage

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:06.980
holds true on a
moment-to-moment basis.

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:09.220
So if the animal's just
kind of generating behavior

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:11.620
and are a particular
instance of a syllable--

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:13.120
say that rear I
showed you-- there's

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:15.310
a high amplitude
dopamine transient, then

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:17.560
that particular rear is
going to be used more

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:21.310
for about the next 3 minutes, as
shown here by this orange line.

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:23.860
And if that very same rear
on some different instance

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:26.860
is associated with a relatively
low dopamine transient, then

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:28.870
over the next 3 minutes,
that specific syllable

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:30.670
will be used less.

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:33.490
Now, dopamine, of
course, is not just

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:34.870
important to reinforcement.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:36.820
It's important to
action selection

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:39.495
in a whole variety of different
contexts that has been shown.

00:20:39.495 --> 00:20:40.870
And so we wondered
whether or not

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:42.787
these dopamine transients
that were associated

00:20:42.787 --> 00:20:45.100
with individual
syllables played any role

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:48.100
in determining the types of
sequences that syllables were

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:49.210
embedded in.

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:53.920
And so we just asked when, on
average, were those syllables

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:57.280
that were associated with
high levels of dopamine,

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:00.280
did they tend to be embedded
in more predictable sequences

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:01.960
or more random sequences?

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:04.023
And it turns out,
as you can see here,

00:21:04.023 --> 00:21:06.190
that there's a nice positive
correlation between how

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:08.020
much dopamine on
average is associated

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:10.630
with a syllable
and the variability

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:14.560
of the behavioral choices
that animals would make

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:16.713
in the future,
such that syllables

00:21:16.713 --> 00:21:18.130
associated with
high dopamine tend

00:21:18.130 --> 00:21:20.860
to be embedded in more
variable behavioral sequences

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:22.990
and syllables that are
associated with low dopamine

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:26.170
tend to be expressed as part
of more predictable, less

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:28.212
variable, less
entropic sequences.

00:21:28.212 --> 00:21:29.920
And again, this effect
seems to hold true

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:31.360
on a moment-by-moment basis.

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:33.907
If you're expressing
that rearing syllable

00:21:33.907 --> 00:21:36.490
I was mentioning to you before
and you happen to be associated

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:39.910
with a high amplitude dopamine
peak, the next two, three,

00:21:39.910 --> 00:21:41.620
or four behavioral
choices you make

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:43.000
tend to be more variable ones.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:46.330
You tend to make a kind of more
unusual next behavioral choice,

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:48.610
as shown here by
this orange line.

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:51.820
And conversely,
if that same rear

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:54.400
is associated with relatively
low levels of dopamine,

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:56.770
the next thing you do tends
to be more predictable,

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:58.330
kind of more ho hum.

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:00.580
So now I've described to
you three things that appear

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:03.250
to be correlated with dopamine.

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:07.210
Those three things are
the animal's velocity,

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:09.640
the usage of individual
syllables on timescales

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.640
of minutes, and
behavioral variability

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:14.680
in terms of sequence
variability evolving

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:15.880
on timescales of seconds.

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:18.280
And we've built
encoding and decoding

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:20.778
models that kind of validate
all the correlations

00:22:20.778 --> 00:22:21.820
that I've just described.

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:23.927
I'm not going to show
you any of those models.

00:22:23.927 --> 00:22:25.510
Instead, I want to
turn to our attempt

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:28.000
to do a causal experiment.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.430
What we wanted to do was to
somehow manipulate dopamine

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:32.950
specifically during the
expression of a given syllable

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:36.670
to ask whether that manipulation
would affect velocity, usage,

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:37.555
or variability.

00:22:37.555 --> 00:22:39.430
So in order to do this,
we've taken advantage

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:42.800
of the magic of deep learning,
which allows us now to render

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:44.720
emotion sequencing closed loop.

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:46.630
So now we can look
down at animals freely

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:49.210
behaving and just running
around in a bucket,

00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:51.190
and I can tell you
with about a 50

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:54.250
millisecond latency or so which
specific behavioral syllable

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:56.293
the animal's expressing
at any moment in time.

00:22:56.293 --> 00:22:58.210
And based upon which
syllable it's expressing,

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:00.310
I can pick out a syllable
I want to manipulate

00:23:00.310 --> 00:23:02.450
and I can send out an
optogenetic trigger.

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:04.930
And here we're going to use
those optogenetic triggers

00:23:04.930 --> 00:23:09.310
to specifically cause dopamine
release in dopaminergic axons

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:12.530
that live within the
dorsolateral striatum itself.

00:23:12.530 --> 00:23:15.130
So it's a very spatially
localized manipulation

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:15.945
of dopamine.

00:23:15.945 --> 00:23:18.070
And here we're going to do
a kind of unusual thing.

00:23:18.070 --> 00:23:21.953
We're going to calibrate our
optogenetic manipulation such

00:23:21.953 --> 00:23:24.370
that the amplitude of dopamine
release that we observe out

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:26.580
here roughly matches
the amplitude

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:29.080
of dopamine transients that we
observe when animals are just

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:30.400
spontaneously behaving.

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:32.470
And practically,
what this means is

00:23:32.470 --> 00:23:35.620
that we're giving a single
200-millisecond long continuous

00:23:35.620 --> 00:23:39.550
wave pulse of light at
extraordinarily low powers.

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:41.987
And what that in turn means
is that this dopamine--

00:23:41.987 --> 00:23:44.320
this kind of optogenetic
dopaminergic manipulation we're

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:46.630
making, which is
spatially localized,

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:50.680
is far more subtle in
general than the sorts

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:52.180
of dopaminergic
manipulations people

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:54.850
have made to implicate
dopamine in behavior.

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:57.820
And so it stood to
reason and we anticipated

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:00.370
that this kind of manipulation
should not change the animal's

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:01.720
behavior at all.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:05.030
And indeed, if we measure
kinematic parameters,

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:08.240
either within a given syllable
or throughout the experiment,

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:10.940
we see that these kinds of
calibrated manipulations

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:13.190
in which we're manipulating
dopamine at the same scale

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:14.930
as dopamine is
naturally fluctuating

00:24:14.930 --> 00:24:18.050
have essentially no
consequence on the animal's

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:19.190
ongoing movements.

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:21.650
These dopamine transients
that we're inducing

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:22.950
don't initiate new movements.

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:24.650
They don't change the
kinematics of syllables.

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:27.020
They don't change the animal
speed or force it to turn.

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:29.960
In other words,
these results suggest

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:33.290
that these natural
fluctuations in dopamine

00:24:33.290 --> 00:24:35.030
that accompany
spontaneous behavior

00:24:35.030 --> 00:24:37.160
are almost certainly
not causal, at least

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:39.860
in dorsolateral striatum,
for influencing the animal's

00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:41.120
ongoing kinematics.

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:43.963
And we know this isn't just an
artifact of our optogenetics

00:24:43.963 --> 00:24:46.130
not working because if we
just turn the light on now

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:48.047
for 2 seconds instead
of 200 milliseconds, now

00:24:48.047 --> 00:24:50.360
we see the usual and
expected dramatic changes

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:53.780
in the animal's movement
in this other context.

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:56.240
So it looks like these
spontaneous fluctuations

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:59.030
aren't changing the
animal's ongoing kinematics.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:01.575
What about how often
syllables are used?

00:25:01.575 --> 00:25:03.200
So here we see
something very dramatic.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:05.543
So here we're going to
pick one syllable out

00:25:05.543 --> 00:25:07.460
of maybe eight or nine
that we've specifically

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:10.490
targeted for optogenetic
pairing and reinforcement,

00:25:10.490 --> 00:25:12.290
here looking at some
pre-stimulation data

00:25:12.290 --> 00:25:13.850
just to look at
baseline expression

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:16.400
of this particular syllable,
which is the rearing syllable.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:18.630
I'm showing it to
you here in green.

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:20.930
And what see is that
nearly immediately,

00:25:20.930 --> 00:25:23.630
as soon as we turn on
the light and begin

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:26.480
to pair the expression
of that rearing

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:30.080
syllable with dopamine,
within two or three pulses,

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:31.828
the animal recognizes
that there's

00:25:31.828 --> 00:25:33.620
some sort of relationship
between this very

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:35.660
subtle optogenetic
manipulation we're making

00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:38.540
and the generation of that
particular egocentric behavior.

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:42.110
And it begins nearly immediately
to upregulate the expression

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:43.370
of that targeted behavior.

00:25:43.370 --> 00:25:44.870
It does it during
the first session.

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:46.328
We give it a little
bit of a break.

00:25:46.328 --> 00:25:48.690
It does it again during
a second session.

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:50.497
And we know that this
isn't just dopamine

00:25:50.497 --> 00:25:52.580
causing the upregulation
of the targeted behavior,

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:54.540
but instead it
reflects learning.

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:57.320
And we know that because
the latency for the animal

00:25:57.320 --> 00:26:00.020
to express the targeted
syllable on the second session

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:01.700
is shorter than on
the first session.

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:03.980
And importantly, if
we turn the light off,

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:06.500
we see this kind of
persistent expression

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:08.660
of the targeted
behavioral syllable,

00:26:08.660 --> 00:26:11.780
even in the absence of this
optogenetic manipulation,

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:13.395
consistent with
the animal having

00:26:13.395 --> 00:26:15.020
learned an association
between dopamine

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:16.700
and this particular
behavior, again

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:17.995
consistent with reinforcement.

00:26:17.995 --> 00:26:19.370
And I just want
to mention what I

00:26:19.370 --> 00:26:23.145
think is kind of amazing here,
which is that, of course,

00:26:23.145 --> 00:26:25.520
while we're doing this, the
animal is just running around

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:28.190
in a dark, empty bucket,
with no sensory cues

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:29.850
from the outside world at all.

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:32.810
So the only way the animal has
to know that it's generating

00:26:32.810 --> 00:26:34.640
the targeted
behavioral syllable is

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:36.680
through [INAUDIBLE]
copies and proprioception,

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:38.390
but that is sufficient
for the animal

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:41.690
to understand its own behavior
and to cause this kind

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:44.120
of targeted upregulation.

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:46.070
What about behavioral
variability?

00:26:46.070 --> 00:26:48.920
We can look at trials in
which we add a little dopamine

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:52.100
and compare the animal's
subsequent behavioral decisions

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:53.600
to what we observed
on those trials.

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:55.100
And we didn't add
a little dopamine.

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:57.260
What we see is consistent
with what I showed you

00:26:57.260 --> 00:27:00.020
in the endogenous data, which
is that on those trials in which

00:27:00.020 --> 00:27:01.670
we add a little bit
of extra dopamine

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:04.400
to a given behavioral syllable,
the very next behavior

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:07.660
the animal generates tends to be
a less likely or more variable

00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:10.160
behavioral choice, whereas on
those [INAUDIBLE] trials where

00:27:10.160 --> 00:27:11.577
we don't do that,
the animal tends

00:27:11.577 --> 00:27:13.222
to do more predictable things.

00:27:13.222 --> 00:27:14.930
And so it's clear from
what I've told you

00:27:14.930 --> 00:27:18.110
that these kind of spontaneous
dopamine fluctuations

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:19.790
don't seem to be
influencing kinematics,

00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:23.060
but instead seem to be
shaping the ongoing structure

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.190
of behavior, both by
influencing behavioral choices

00:27:25.190 --> 00:27:27.930
in the near future, and I
think, most importantly,

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:30.170
by reinforcing over
time scales of minutes

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:33.020
the usage of associated
behavioral syllables.

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:36.200
And this suggests that maybe
these spontaneous dopamine

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:37.730
fluctuations that
we're observing

00:27:37.730 --> 00:27:40.730
are acting as a kind of
continuous teaching signal

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:44.000
to sculpt the
architecture of behavior.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:45.840
So we wanted to kind
of query that formally.

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:49.160
And so to do that, we've
built a simple, very simple

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:52.070
reinforcing learning model,
reinforcement learning model,

00:27:52.070 --> 00:27:56.690
in which we use actual data,
actual dopamine syllable

00:27:56.690 --> 00:28:00.290
associations, to train a
reinforcement learning agent.

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:03.500
And then we ask whether or not
the behavioral choices that

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.810
are made by this trained
reinforcement learning agent

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:08.240
match up with kind
of held out observed

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:10.513
behavioral choices that
are made by real-life mice.

00:28:10.513 --> 00:28:11.930
And if, in fact,
they match up, it

00:28:11.930 --> 00:28:14.635
would suggest that,
in fact, dopamine

00:28:14.635 --> 00:28:16.260
is acting as a kind
of teaching signal.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:19.700
So here, just to quickly
explain how the model works,

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:22.040
the state that the model is
in represents the syllable

00:28:22.040 --> 00:28:23.750
the animal's expressing
at any moment in time.

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:26.042
The action is the animal's
choice of its next syllable.

00:28:26.042 --> 00:28:28.850
Here we, of course, substitute
the traditional reward signal

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:32.360
for the dopamine that's
associated with any given

00:28:32.360 --> 00:28:33.500
syllable.

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:35.330
And we use simple
cue learning rules

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:37.432
to update a cue
table, which here

00:28:37.432 --> 00:28:39.140
is equivalent to a
transition matrix that

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:41.390
governs how any
syllable transitions

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:42.482
into any other syllable.

00:28:42.482 --> 00:28:43.940
You'll notice here
we're only going

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:46.648
to focus on transitions
amongst the top 10 syllables.

00:28:46.648 --> 00:28:48.440
That's because we're
a little data limited.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:49.760
And you'll notice
also that there's

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:51.530
some syllable transitions
we don't consider,

00:28:51.530 --> 00:28:54.030
and that's because these are
syllable transitions that don't

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:55.370
exist in the empirical data.

00:28:55.370 --> 00:28:58.820
That said, we initialize this
transition matrix uniformly,

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:01.850
and then we just kind of train
a reinforcement learning agent

00:29:01.850 --> 00:29:03.350
using simple cue learning rules.

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:05.770
And we can learn a transition
matrix that effectively

00:29:05.770 --> 00:29:09.070
reflects the behavioral choices
this reinforcement learning

00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:12.070
agent would make if it were able
to behave in the real world,

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:14.080
and then we can compare
this high-dimensional--

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:17.650
this 100-dimensional transition
matrix to an actual transition

00:29:17.650 --> 00:29:19.480
matrix from held out data.

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:21.160
And what's remarkable
is that we capture

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:25.120
a huge amount of the
behavioral variation that's

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:27.640
observed in real-life
data when we just

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:31.400
train an RL agent using these
syllable-associated dopamine

00:29:31.400 --> 00:29:31.900
transients.

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:35.498
And that really argues both
that continuous variation

00:29:35.498 --> 00:29:37.540
in dopamine might be acting
as a teaching signal,

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:41.140
and that those signals might
be interpreted by animals

00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:43.360
as reward-like signals,

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:44.860
On this last data
slide, I just want

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:46.360
to mention that
everything that I've

00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:48.700
shown you here is specific
to dorsolateral striatum.

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:50.768
If we go ahead and repeat
the exact experiments

00:29:50.768 --> 00:29:52.810
I've been describing to
you dorsomedial striatum,

00:29:52.810 --> 00:29:54.280
we no longer see
the correlations

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:56.890
between dopamine and behavior
that I described to you before.

00:29:56.890 --> 00:29:58.723
And when we try to
optogenetically stimulate

00:29:58.723 --> 00:30:00.640
dorsomedial striatum,
now we can no longer

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:03.250
reinforce individual
behavioral syllables.

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:05.350
So to sum up, I've
described to you

00:30:05.350 --> 00:30:08.230
at a kind of very high-level
motion sequencing, which

00:30:08.230 --> 00:30:12.100
allows us to atomize behavior
into components, thereby

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:13.630
revealing this
kind of underlying

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:15.130
structure to behavior.

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:17.680
I've told you that it seems
like dorsolateral striatum plays

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:20.590
an important role in assembling
the behavioral sequences that

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:22.630
are captured by this
behavioral state map,

00:30:22.630 --> 00:30:25.810
and that dopamine seems to play
an essential role in affording

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:28.840
this behavioral state
map its structure.

00:30:28.840 --> 00:30:31.225
It does so not through
controlling the animal's

00:30:31.225 --> 00:30:33.100
kinematics on a
moment-to-moment basis, which

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:35.080
is, I think, the kind
of naive, [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:35.080 --> 00:30:37.000
prediction you would
have made, but instead,

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:38.500
in this kind of
very surprising way,

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:41.650
by acting as a teaching signal
to shape the future usage

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:44.170
and sequencing of
behavioral elements

00:30:44.170 --> 00:30:45.790
during spontaneous behavior.

00:30:45.790 --> 00:30:48.580
And what's really gratifying
about this is that, of course,

00:30:48.580 --> 00:30:50.500
this means that the
role that dopamine

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:52.210
is playing during
spontaneous behavior

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:54.640
is analogous to the kind
of role that dopamine

00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:57.160
has been well
characterized as playing

00:30:57.160 --> 00:30:58.810
during much more
structured tasks

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:00.010
when animals have rewards.

00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:02.645
It looks like the very
same kind of circuits

00:31:02.645 --> 00:31:04.270
and in fact, the very
same computations

00:31:04.270 --> 00:31:06.910
that support kind of
reward-based, goal-oriented

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:09.880
learning seem to come into play
during spontaneous behavior,

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:12.880
suggesting a kind of unitary
function for dopamine

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:15.190
in controlling the overall
architecture of behavior

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:16.420
in multiple circumstances.

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:18.970
And the key remaining
question is, of course, why

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:20.440
does dopamine vary?

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:22.780
What's controlling the
variation in dopamine,

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:24.640
thereby structuring behavior?

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:27.123
That's a problem
for another day.

00:31:27.123 --> 00:31:28.540
And so with that
I'll just mention

00:31:28.540 --> 00:31:29.740
that the work that
I've showed you today

00:31:29.740 --> 00:31:31.600
was largely done by Jeff
Markowitz, who just started

00:31:31.600 --> 00:31:33.670
his own lab of Georgia Tech,
along with two great graduate

00:31:33.670 --> 00:31:35.260
students, Wen
Gillis and Maya Jay.

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:37.030
And all this work is part
of a broad collaboration

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:39.460
I have with Bernardo Sabatini,
Scott Linderman, and now

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:39.967
Uchida.

00:31:39.967 --> 00:31:41.050
Thanks for your attention.

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:42.982
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:50.395 --> 00:31:52.270
SPEAKER: We have a few
minutes for questions.

00:31:57.400 --> 00:32:04.225
AUDIENCE: When you showed
[INAUDIBLE] syllable

00:32:04.225 --> 00:32:06.610
[INAUDIBLE].

00:32:06.610 --> 00:32:07.270
BOB DATTA: Yes.

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:10.470
AUDIENCE: I was
doing a [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:12.730
where they come from
and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:13.400
BOB DATTA: That's
a good question.

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.520
So the reason why we built
these encoding and decoding

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:16.960
models was to get a
sense of-- there's

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:19.002
going to be some correlation
amongst all of them.

00:32:19.002 --> 00:32:20.570
The correlations
are pretty weak.

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:23.260
But we wanted to get a sense of,
if we were to build an encoding

00:32:23.260 --> 00:32:25.330
model, for example, or
a decoding model, what's

00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:28.000
the relative weight afforded
to each one of these kind

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:30.260
of behavioral features, if
you wanted to, for example,

00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:33.170
predict the fluctuations
in dopamine?

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.530
So it turns out that each
one of these three things

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:42.790
seems to play a different and
complementary role in kind

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:44.530
of recovering the
dopamine transients.

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:46.318
So all three seem to matter.

00:32:46.318 --> 00:32:47.860
And what's interesting
is that if you

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:52.840
look at the time scales at which
the beta parameters evolve,

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:55.810
it turns out that velocity
is important at very, very

00:32:55.810 --> 00:32:58.838
short time scales along with
sequence variability, which

00:32:58.838 --> 00:33:00.880
is exactly what I showed
you in the correlations.

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.670
And usage tends to be
important over much longer time

00:33:03.670 --> 00:33:05.810
scales, which is exactly
what I showed you.

00:33:05.810 --> 00:33:07.990
Usage seems to be
reinforced for minutes,

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:09.730
whereas variability
and velocity seem

00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:11.900
to be important over seconds.

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:14.500
And actually, the models
capture those temporal dynamics

00:33:14.500 --> 00:33:16.408
and demonstrate
that it looks like,

00:33:16.408 --> 00:33:18.700
if you wanted to predict
dopamine, you'd need all three

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:19.900
of these different
behavioral features

00:33:19.900 --> 00:33:21.310
to make accurate predictions.

00:33:24.165 --> 00:33:24.790
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:33:24.790 --> 00:33:26.590
That was amazing
how you pin down

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:29.680
these relationships between
dopamine and the syllables.

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:32.920
I'm wondering,
though, is this kind

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:34.810
of dopamine noise, or
reward noise that's

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:37.060
happening in these situations
where the animal is just

00:33:37.060 --> 00:33:38.650
walking around and
you have these kind

00:33:38.650 --> 00:33:43.960
of micro unmotivated or
unexplained fluctuations

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:47.170
that have no purpose, and the
animal's behavior is modulated

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:49.083
by it but not in any
purposeful or good way?

00:33:49.083 --> 00:33:49.750
BOB DATTA: Yeah.

00:33:49.750 --> 00:33:51.850
So in my discussion, which--

00:33:51.850 --> 00:33:53.500
there are many
possibilities for where

00:33:53.500 --> 00:33:56.350
these dopamine
fluctuations arise.

00:33:56.350 --> 00:33:59.800
And in fact, David
Kleinfeld has recently

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:03.280
published a paper in which
essentially stochastic dopamine

00:34:03.280 --> 00:34:05.703
fluctuations in the cortex,
over much longer time

00:34:05.703 --> 00:34:07.120
scales that I'm
showing you here--

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:08.710
these are subsecond
fluctuations.

00:34:08.710 --> 00:34:10.750
His fluctuations are
over many minutes.

00:34:10.750 --> 00:34:13.090
But high-amplitude dopamine
fluctuations in the cortex

00:34:13.090 --> 00:34:15.310
are seemingly appropriated,
these random fluctuations

00:34:15.310 --> 00:34:16.840
are seemingly
appropriated as kind

00:34:16.840 --> 00:34:19.090
of windows of learning that
animals can take advantage

00:34:19.090 --> 00:34:21.909
of that shape behavior in
this kind of accidental way.

00:34:21.909 --> 00:34:23.793
We think that this
is not accidental

00:34:23.793 --> 00:34:25.960
and we have ideas about
where these fluctuations are

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:27.080
coming from.

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:31.034
And the reason we think it's
not accidental is because--

00:34:31.034 --> 00:34:33.159
I think I kind of didn't
have time to elaborate on,

00:34:33.159 --> 00:34:34.480
but which, I think,
when you think about it,

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:36.855
is pretty obvious, is when
you throw a mouse in a bucket,

00:34:36.855 --> 00:34:40.210
actually, it has
characteristic and stereotyped

00:34:40.210 --> 00:34:43.449
behavioral dynamics that
evolve in a really reliable way

00:34:43.449 --> 00:34:45.030
across animals, across time.

00:34:45.030 --> 00:34:46.780
So when animals first
get into the bucket,

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:48.010
they're in this kind
of exploration mode

00:34:48.010 --> 00:34:50.110
and they kind of are in this
like less exploratory mode,

00:34:50.110 --> 00:34:51.250
and then they're in this
kind of chill out mode.

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:53.010
And that's all very stereotyped.

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:54.909
So the fact that
behavior itself has

00:34:54.909 --> 00:34:57.070
very stereotyped
temporal dynamics

00:34:57.070 --> 00:34:59.740
is difficult to
reconcile between--

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:01.505
and if dopamine is
in fact, reinforcing,

00:35:01.505 --> 00:35:03.380
it's difficult to
reconcile those two things.

00:35:03.380 --> 00:35:04.870
And so we think it's not noise.

00:35:04.870 --> 00:35:06.120
I mean, formally, it could be.

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:08.472
It's choice number 3 in our
discussion of possibilities.

00:35:08.472 --> 00:35:10.180
But I think it's likely
to be something--

00:35:10.180 --> 00:35:13.870
we think it's likely to
be the output of some sort

00:35:13.870 --> 00:35:16.785
of computation that's actually
shaping the animal's behavior.

00:35:16.785 --> 00:35:18.160
And if you'd like
me to speculate

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:20.335
on what I think that
computation is, I'd be glad to.

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:26.180
AUDIENCE: I was intrigued
by your observations

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:30.260
of dopamine dips around the
transition points of syllables.

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:34.290
And it sort of aligned with a
question that I generally have,

00:35:34.290 --> 00:35:37.490
which is that it seems like
you're treating dopamine

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:42.680
as a sort of prospective
signal, rather than

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:45.373
looking at it as sort of
a retrospective signal.

00:35:45.373 --> 00:35:46.790
And I'm wondering
if you've looked

00:35:46.790 --> 00:35:52.340
at explicitly the stoppage
of syllables associated

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:57.440
with the dip, since one syllable
stops before another begins,

00:35:57.440 --> 00:35:59.930
and sort of related
to that, if you,

00:35:59.930 --> 00:36:02.420
with your optogenetic
stimulation, if you

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:08.030
look at what's happening
before your stimulation.

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:09.690
I realize you're
doing this in closed

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:11.690
loop with a specific
syllable, so it makes sense

00:36:11.690 --> 00:36:13.730
that one is reinforced.

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:17.390
But I guess I'm wondering
about your framework

00:36:17.390 --> 00:36:19.253
and thinking about it
sort of prospectively

00:36:19.253 --> 00:36:20.420
rather than retrospectively.

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:22.125
BOB DATTA: So you
might imagine that

00:36:22.125 --> 00:36:23.750
either just in the
endogenous data when

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:27.570
we look at correlations,
or in the optogenetic data,

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:31.850
there might be some temporal
slop, that maybe when

00:36:31.850 --> 00:36:33.477
I reinforce a syllable,
it doesn't just

00:36:33.477 --> 00:36:35.060
reinforce that
syllable, but you might

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:36.860
expect that it would
reinforce the behavior that

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:38.402
led to that syllable
and the syllable

00:36:38.402 --> 00:36:40.160
before, retrospectively.

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:41.990
Or you might expect
that it could maybe

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:45.120
be a teaching signal in the
kind of anterior direction.

00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:48.200
So we've done all of the sorts
of things you'd want us to do.

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:52.487
We've taken the dopamine
signals and lagged them out,

00:36:52.487 --> 00:36:54.320
forward and backwards,
to see whether or not

00:36:54.320 --> 00:36:56.090
the correlations still hold.

00:36:56.090 --> 00:36:59.040
In all of our models, we do
that as routinely as a control.

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:03.740
So it turns out that the
reinforcement that we're

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:06.770
predicting based upon an
endogenous dopamine fluctuation

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:08.180
really seems like
it's temporally

00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:10.940
associated with the syllable
that it's attached to.

00:37:10.940 --> 00:37:13.118
There is a little
bit of slop, maybe.

00:37:13.118 --> 00:37:14.660
The amount of
reinforcement goes down

00:37:14.660 --> 00:37:19.850
by a factor of a log to the two
temporally adjacent syllables,

00:37:19.850 --> 00:37:20.480
and that's it.

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:22.100
We can do the same thing in the
reinforcement learning model.

00:37:22.100 --> 00:37:23.870
I showed you this beautiful
transition matrix.

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:26.162
If we just shift over the
relationship between behavior

00:37:26.162 --> 00:37:28.580
and dopamine by one
syllable, that relationship

00:37:28.580 --> 00:37:29.750
goes to almost nothing.

00:37:29.750 --> 00:37:31.968
So it really is quite
precise, actually,

00:37:31.968 --> 00:37:34.010
which is a little bit
surprising and interesting.

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:37.730
I'll say that we've built a
model in which a prediction you

00:37:37.730 --> 00:37:42.110
might make is that those
syllables that on average are

00:37:42.110 --> 00:37:43.880
associated with
the most dopamine

00:37:43.880 --> 00:37:45.890
should be those syllables
that show the greatest

00:37:45.890 --> 00:37:49.820
degree of reinforcement, if our
exogenous optogenetics is kind

00:37:49.820 --> 00:37:52.340
of being added to the
endogenous fluctuations.

00:37:52.340 --> 00:37:54.770
That prediction turns out
to be true, again, kind

00:37:54.770 --> 00:37:57.420
of centering our
attention on the syllable

00:37:57.420 --> 00:37:58.670
that we're actually targeting.

00:37:58.670 --> 00:37:59.378
But you're right.

00:37:59.378 --> 00:38:01.258
There is some slop
in both directions.

00:38:01.258 --> 00:38:03.050
There's a little bit
of retrospective slop,

00:38:03.050 --> 00:38:05.630
a little bit of
prospective slop.

00:38:05.630 --> 00:38:08.480
I also mentioned-- sorry,
sometimes I get to talk.

00:38:08.480 --> 00:38:10.400
I'll also mention one
thing about this, which

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:12.080
I think is really interesting.

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:14.270
So the duration distribution
of the syllables

00:38:14.270 --> 00:38:16.530
has a mean that's 400
or 500 milliseconds,

00:38:16.530 --> 00:38:18.380
but the actual
implementation of syllables

00:38:18.380 --> 00:38:19.830
is often much longer than that.

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:22.220
And what that means is that
we're shutting the light off

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:23.928
and the dopamine
transient that we induce

00:38:23.928 --> 00:38:28.540
is off nearly always before
the syllable is completed.

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:31.100
And yet syllables stay intact.

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:34.390
So if behavior were truly fully
continuous and not discretized

00:38:34.390 --> 00:38:37.000
in any way, you might imagine
that we would be reinforcing

00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:39.040
just the front half of
each syllable, or just

00:38:39.040 --> 00:38:39.910
the part of the
syllable that was

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:41.890
associated with our
optogenetic manipulation.

00:38:41.890 --> 00:38:43.330
But actually, that's not true.

00:38:43.330 --> 00:38:46.000
Syllable durations
don't change at all

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:48.310
when we do this optogenetic
manipulation, only

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:49.270
usages change.

00:38:49.270 --> 00:38:51.520
And that suggests this
kind of intriguing idea

00:38:51.520 --> 00:38:53.700
that maybe there's something
discrete about behavior

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:56.200
at this time scale, which is
very consistent with the things

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:58.200
that you were talking
about earlier today, which

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:00.408
I love that work about
autocorrelation across cortex.

00:39:00.408 --> 00:39:02.450
And I very much have been
thinking about the idea

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:04.210
that maybe those
kinds of time scales

00:39:04.210 --> 00:39:06.288
are setting these
discrete time scales that

00:39:06.288 --> 00:39:08.080
are used by the brain
to organize sensation

00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:11.390
and action into little bundles
that make sense to the brain.

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:14.275
That's a speculative idea
we can talk about at dinner.

00:39:14.275 --> 00:39:14.900
SPEAKER: Great.

00:39:14.900 --> 00:39:15.640
Fantastic.

00:39:15.640 --> 00:39:16.840
I'm sorry.

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:19.730
Let's do it at poster time
because it's poster session.

00:39:19.730 --> 00:39:22.880
So please hold your questions
and bring them to Bob.

00:39:22.880 --> 00:39:25.500
[APPLAUSE]

